body:
Closes #164319

## What Problem This Solves

`openclaw doctor --fix` can reject its required pre-migration SQLite snapshot on Linux hosts without `statx`. Thanks @StevenKrebs for reporting this and @jayzhou2309 for the original fix and Linux reproduction.

## User Impact

Doctor can publish unchanged rollback backups on these hosts. Changed file identities, sizes, or database bytes still fail verification.

## Why This Change Was Made

Node/libuv can substitute ctime when the filesystem cannot report birth time. Removing the original hard-link name changes ctime, so comparing the publication receipt's birth time to a later stat can reject the same unchanged inode.

The intermediate transfer fingerprint now compares only device/inode identity and size on every platform. The snapshot owner retains its content hashes before and after publication. Coverage uses real hard-link publication with zero and ctime-derived birth times, checks database contents and cleanup, and rejects actual same-size byte changes instead of treating an mtime-only change as rewritten data.

## Evidence

Follow-up `284bd601a89fb3923ca21e98085944b1fff9eb26` adds one commit on top of the contributor's `b9d53bae7298bcef61359cb299c57496adfb54ee`; their two commits and PR authorship are preserved. Local verification on the final source:

- Transfer suite: **11/11 passed three times**, using `node scripts/run-vitest.mjs src/infra/sqlite-snapshot.transfer.test.ts --maxWorkers=1` (the repository runner used by `pnpm test`). Runner wall time: **89.08 / 38.33 / 59.72 seconds**; summed test-case time: **10.289 / 10.673 / 11.670 seconds**. Compiler preparation accounts for most cold-run overhead.
- Snapshot, FUSE, file-identity, and Doctor migration-backup suites: **63 passed, 2 existing skips**.
- `test/scripts/eager-import-closure.test.ts` and `test/scripts/pr-wrapper-source-closure.test.ts`: **20/20 passed**, including the PR wrapper inventory check.
- `CI=1 OPENCLAW_LOCAL_CHECK=1 node scripts/check-changed.mjs --base 86e3f23b2418033faae116b8c2b6e88453c960dc`: **exit 0**, including core and all 27 test typecheck graphs, lint, ratchets, and static guards. `git diff --check` also passed.
- Codex P1 review was attempted but returned **HTTP 401 Unauthorized**; no automated review verdict was produced. New-head hosted CI is not included in these local results; earlier contributor CI evidence is below.

All stateful proof used isolated test state. No module, schema, configuration, or dependency changes were introduced. Auto-merge remains off.

Before this follow-up, an isolated regression on main `678572811dd8b10c19ee69e2107947d099dd33c1` produced 1 failed / 9 passed at the reported staging-transfer guard; the device/inode/size repair passed 10/10 three times. A real hard-link/unlink probe using the extracted `v2026.9.8` guard also rejected ctime-as-birthtime while device, inode, size, mtime, and SHA-256 remained unchanged. Stable zero birth time passed. This was stat simulation, not a Linux 4.4 machine or a full historical installation upgrade. The final contributor-branch suite adds the real byte-mutation negative control and contains 11 tests.

<details>
<summary>Contributor's earlier validation and Linux proof on the named prior heads</summary>

- Rebased on 2026-10-04 onto upstream/main 86e3f23b241, because #164628 rewrote this test file (dropped `expectSnapshotSuccess`, merged the copy-fallback case into the publish `it.each`). The new tests are unchanged except that the success case checks the receipt inline. The fix commit is b9d53bae729, and its source diff is byte-identical to 946d8607516, so the Linux proof below still applies. On the new base: test commit 52db4076bde gives 1 failed, 9 passed, and the fix gives 10/10. All 18 `src/infra/sqlite-snapshot*.test.ts` files pass (122 passed, 2 skipped). `tsgo:core` and `tsgo:core:test` exit 0. `run-oxlint` exits 0, and `oxfmt --check` is clean on both files.
- Red first. Test commit 52db4076bde (d74ba079fb7 before the rebase) adds two cases to `src/infra/sqlite-snapshot.transfer.test.ts`. Both run a real hard-link transfer, then report `process.platform` as `linux` and birthtime as ctime for the staging file and its receipt, as Node does without `statx`. "accepts a hard-link transfer when Linux reports ctime as birthtime" fails on upstream/main d41a61f9b80 with the reported `SQLite snapshot staging file changed during transfer` from `sqlite-snapshot.ts:392`, and passes on the fix. "rejects a staged image rewritten during a Linux hard-link transfer" changes the staged file's mtime mid-transfer and is rejected on both.
- `pnpm test src/infra/*sqlite-snapshot*.test.ts`: 19 files, 134 passed, 3 skipped. `pnpm tsgo:core` exits 0, and the test typecheck shard that covers these files (`test/tsconfig/tsconfig.core.test.state-logging.json`) exits 0. `scripts/run-oxlint.mjs` exit 0 and `oxfmt --check` clean on the 2 changed files.

### Real behavior proof

Doctor's pre-migration backup runs on real Linux from source, on upstream/main d41a61f9b80 (before) and on this branch 946d8607516 (after). The script calls the same code `openclaw doctor --fix` uses for its rollback backup: it takes the real Doctor maintenance lock (`withDoctorSqliteMaintenanceLock`) and runs the Doctor backup owner (`backupDoctorSqliteDatabases`) on a fresh shared state database created by `openOpenClawStateDatabase`. That reaches `createVerifiedSqliteSnapshot` and the hard-link publication. Nothing is mocked: no `process.platform` override and no stat stubs. It does not run the full `openclaw doctor --fix` CLI, and the database is a new one rather than an upgrade from 2026.9.4 with a pending schema migration.

Setup: Docker on an arm64 Mac (Linux kernel 7.0.12-linuxkit, the `node:24-bookworm-slim` image, Node v24.21.0, the version in the issue). Both revisions are bundled with esbuild from the repo source, along with their `infra/*` worker entrypoints. This kernel has `statx`, so the "no statx" condition from the issue is made with a seccomp profile. It returns `ENOSYS` for `statx` calls that use libuv's mask (`0xFFF`, basic stats plus btime), and for `io_uring_setup`. That is the result libuv gets on Linux 4.4. libuv then falls back to plain `stat` and fills birthtime from ctime (`deps/uv/src/unix/fs.c`, the `uv__fs_statx` fallback). The filter matches only that mask because runc itself calls `statx` with `STATX_MNT_ID` during container start.

```json
{"defaultAction":"SCMP_ACT_ALLOW","syscalls":[
 {"names":["statx"],"action":"SCMP_ACT_ERRNO","errnoRet":38,"args":[{"index":3,"value":4095,"op":"SCMP_CMP_EQ"}]},
 {"names":["io_uring_setup"],"action":"SCMP_ACT_ERRNO","errnoRet":38}]}
```

Before, on d41a61f9b80, with Node's `statx` blocked, state dir on the container's own filesystem (`$ROOT` replaces the temp path):

```
[base] kernel=7.0.12-linuxkit arch=arm64 node=v24.21.0
[base] stat probe: birthtimeNs==ctimeNs true; birthtime changed across link+unlink true; ctime changed true
[base] state db: $ROOT/state-base/state/openclaw.sqlite (1458176 bytes)
[base] SQLite database cannot be snapshotted safely: $ROOT/state-base/state/openclaw.sqlite. SQLite snapshot staging file changed during transfer: $ROOT/state-base/state/.sqlite-publish-741ba596-82b7-46d3-b825-e65b169b2258-wPYkNs/database.sqlite
[base] RESULT failed
```

After, on 946d8607516, same container setup:

```
[head] kernel=7.0.12-linuxkit arch=arm64 node=v24.21.0
[head] stat probe: birthtimeNs==ctimeNs true; birthtime changed across link+unlink true; ctime changed true
[head] state db: $ROOT/state-head/state/openclaw.sqlite (1458176 bytes)
[head] change: Saved pre-migration SQLite backup: $ROOT/state-head/state/openclaw.sqlite.pre-startup-migration-ff498787-390b-84c7-82b5-0d149acadeb8.bak
[head] RESULT ok
```

The stat probe line comes before the backup. It writes a file, hard-links it, unlinks the original name, and stats the link. With `statx` blocked, birthtime equals ctime and moves when the unlink changes ctime. That is the field main's fingerprint compares.

The full matrix ran twice, with the same results both times:

| Node `statx` | State dir filesystem | birthtime moves on link+unlink | main d41a61f9b80 | branch 946d8607516 |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| blocked (`ENOSYS`) | container filesystem (overlayfs) | yes | **fails**: `staging file changed during transfer` | backup saved |
| blocked (`ENOSYS`) | bind mount from the macOS host (virtiofs) | yes | backup saved | backup saved |
| available | container filesystem (overlayfs) | no | backup saved | backup saved |
| available | bind mount from the macOS host (virtiofs) | no | backup saved | backup saved |

What this shows:

- The failure needs Node to fall back from `statx`. With `statx` available, Linux reports a real birthtime that does not move, and main publishes the backup. So the Linux 4.4 `statx` gap in the issue is the trigger.
- On the bind mount from the macOS host, main did not fail even with `statx` blocked. The cause is not traced. A guess is virtiofs attribute caching hiding the ctime change during the transfer window. The issue's bind mount came from a Linux host, which this setup does not reproduce.
- The branch saved the backup in every case.

Commands, from the repo root on each revision (`git checkout --detach <commit>`):

```
esbuild entry=proof.ts <each infra/*.worker from src/infra/runtime-process-entrypoints.ts> \
  --bundle --platform=node --format=esm --target=node24 --splitting \
  --external:sqlite-vec --outdir=<rev> --out-extension:.js=.mjs   # plus src/state/*.sql copied beside it
docker create --security-opt seccomp=no-statx.json -v "$PWD:/proof:ro" \
  -e PROOF_LABEL=<base|head> -e PROOF_ROOT=/var/oc-proof node:24-bookworm-slim \
  sh -c 'mkdir -p /var/oc-proof && node /proof/<rev>/entry.mjs'
docker start -a <id>
```

`@openclaw/fs-safe@0.23.0` and its `linux-arm64-gnu` native package sit in `node_modules` beside the bundles. Each run takes a few seconds. The proof script is not part of this PR.

<details>
<summary>Proof script (proof.ts)</summary>

```ts
import fs from "node:fs";
import os from "node:os";
import path from "node:path";
import { backupDoctorSqliteDatabases } from "<repo>/src/commands/doctor-migration-backup.ts";
import { withDoctorSqliteMaintenanceLock } from "<repo>/src/commands/doctor-sqlite-maintenance-lock.ts";
import {
  closeOpenClawStateDatabase,
  openOpenClawStateDatabase,
} from "<repo>/src/state/openclaw-state-db.ts";
import { resolveOpenClawStateSqlitePath } from "<repo>/src/state/openclaw-state-db.paths.ts";

const label = process.env.PROOF_LABEL ?? "run";
const root = process.env.PROOF_ROOT!;
const stateDir = path.join(root, `state-${label}`);
fs.rmSync(stateDir, { recursive: true, force: true });
fs.mkdirSync(stateDir, { recursive: true, mode: 0o700 });
const env = { ...process.env, OPENCLAW_STATE_DIR: stateDir, HOME: stateDir };

console.log(`[${label}] kernel=${os.release()} arch=${process.arch} node=${process.version}`);

const probe = path.join(stateDir, "probe.tmp");
const probeLink = path.join(stateDir, "probe.link");
fs.writeFileSync(probe, "x");
const before = fs.statSync(probe, { bigint: true });
fs.linkSync(probe, probeLink);
fs.unlinkSync(probe);
const after = fs.statSync(probeLink, { bigint: true });
fs.unlinkSync(probeLink);
console.log(
  `[${label}] stat probe: birthtimeNs==ctimeNs ${before.birthtimeNs === before.ctimeNs}; ` +
    `birthtime changed across link+unlink ${before.birthtimeNs !== after.birthtimeNs}; ` +
    `ctime changed ${before.ctimeNs !== after.ctimeNs}`,
);

const db = openOpenClawStateDatabase({ env });
void db;
closeOpenClawStateDatabase();
const sharedPath = resolveOpenClawStateSqlitePath(env);
console.log(`[${label}] state db: ${sharedPath.replace(root, "$ROOT")} (${fs.statSync(sharedPath).size} bytes)`);

try {
  const result = await withDoctorSqliteMaintenanceLock({
    env,
    operation: "pre-migration backup proof",
    run: (authority) =>
      backupDoctorSqliteDatabases({
        env,
        pendingDatabasePaths: [sharedPath],
        databasePaths: [sharedPath],
        authority,
      }),
  });
  for (const line of result.changes) console.log(`[${label}] change: ${line.replaceAll(root, "$ROOT")}`);
  for (const line of result.warnings) console.log(`[${label}] warning: ${line.replaceAll(root, "$ROOT")}`);
  console.log(`[${label}] RESULT ok`);
} catch (error) {
  const lines = [String((error as Error).message)];
  for (let c = (error as Error).cause; c; c = (c as Error).cause) lines.push(`cause: ${(c as Error).message}`);
  for (const line of lines) console.log(`[${label}] ${line.replaceAll(root, "$ROOT")}`);
  console.log(`[${label}] RESULT failed`);
  process.exitCode = 1;
}
```

</details>

Not verified: a real Linux 4.4 host. The seccomp filter makes `statx` unavailable to Node on a newer kernel, and the rest of the stack is real: kernel, filesystem, libuv fallback, and the publisher. The issue also notes that the failing run did not record which stat field differed. The probe above shows birthtime is the field that moves under this condition.

### Test cost and exact-head CI

- `pnpm test src/infra/sqlite-snapshot.transfer.test.ts --maxWorkers=1` at 946d8607516 (macOS arm64): 10 tests pass. Wall time was 31.4 s cold (Vitest 28.3 s, 90% transform), then 7.80 s and 7.35 s warm (Vitest 5.95 s and 5.69 s). The two new tests take about 185 ms and 175 ms. They add no timers, sleeps, fixtures, or Gateway boots.
- [CI run 37131972422](https://github.com/openclaw/openclaw/actions/runs/37131972422) on 946d8607516: the file ran in its own shard of [`checks-node-changed-3`](https://github.com/openclaw/openclaw/actions/runs/37131972422/job/111228947308) on a Linux x64 runner. There, 10 tests passed in 3.56 s of Vitest time (4.81 s shard wrapper), and the new tests took 134 ms and 124 ms. The whole job took 2 m 24 s, including other shards.

</details>
